      k: "01",
      h: "Hundreds of applicants per role, sometimes thousands",
      p: "This is the number to absorb before anything else, because it explains almost every other frustration in a search. An ordinary Dutch vacancy draws hundreds of applications. One written broadly, or at a company people want to work for, goes past a thousand. And it happens fast: the great majority arrive in the first days after the posting goes up, so a role opened two weeks ago is already being worked.",
      list: [
        "The first conclusion, and the uncomfortable one: a CV sent through the form was not read by a person. A screening system saw it first, and it looks for textual match, not potential",
        "The second: applying more does not improve your odds, it dilutes them. A hundred average applications are worth less than five precise ones, because breadth cannot be verified and specificity can",
        "The third, and the one the whole method follows from: if the queue at the front door runs to hundreds, the work is to find a different door. An approach that arrives through a person, before the role is posted or somewhere it will never be posted at all, is not standing in that queue",
        "And a rejection with no reply is not a judgement on you. At these volumes an employer who does not answer is the rule rather than the exception. Read nothing into it",
      ],
      note: "These numbers are an estimate from the field, not a figure quoted from a report. They come from what I see at the employers and recruiters I work with, and they move by industry and by season. The order of magnitude is the point here, not a precise number.",
