    },
    {
      k: "02",
      h: "Innovation here is pointed somewhere else",
      p: "Wherever you are arriving from, the industries that are strong there are probably not the industries that are strong here. Dutch innovation concentrates on infrastructure systems, financial systems and banking, energy and life sciences, and smart and sustainable city solutions. Startups in the sense most people mean the word exist here, but they are the minority.",
      list: [
        "Match the vocabulary in your CV to the industries that are genuinely strong here, rather than the ones you are used to writing for",
        "There are international companies operating in the Netherlands that break this pattern entirely. They are worth mapping in their own right",
        "If the pull is really the move to the Netherlands rather than the work being in the Netherlands, remote is worth examining, though the field has been contracting since the pandemic",
      ],
    },
    {
      k: "03",
      h: "Work culture: what feels like a disadvantage is usually the opposite",
      p: "In front of you is a market with a real shortage of local people, a genuine reliance on workers from abroad, very high English, and a strong emphasis on the balance between work and the rest of life. What you bring to it is a strong technical background, a faster pace, and a bias to act. That gap frightens people, and it should not.",
      list: [
        "Being someone who gets things done is a dramatic advantage in the eyes of a Dutch employer. Make it visible in the CV and through the process",
        "A military background, where it is relevant to the role, is not a problem here",
        "Dutch is not required at most employers, but it is a real advantage in non technical roles and at more traditional companies",
        "The Dutch pace can frustrate someone used to working faster. It is also the opportunity to put more into the life outside the job",
      ],
    },
    {
      k: "04",
      h: "The Dutch calendar, and this is the part nobody tells you",
      p: "Hiring here has seasons, and they are fixed and nationally synchronised. Anyone who does not count backwards from them finds out too late that a whole quarter is gone.",
      list: [
        "A senior hiring process takes six to ten weeks, several rounds and sometimes a take home assignment",
        "Signing is not starting: under Dutch law a resignation takes effect on the first of the following month, and for senior people the notice period is usually two months",
        "On 31 August the entire country comes back to the desk at once, and September is the busiest month of the year for applications",
        "From 19 December the country is off, it returns slowly in mid January, and hiring processes only really start moving again in February",
      ],
    },
    {
      k: "05",
      h: "Look for companies, not for jobs",
      p: "This is the practical conclusion from everything above, and it is also the method I work by. When you fire in every direction the resource you burn is attention, and every effort comes at the cost of another one.",
      list: [
        "Be stingy in a CV. Its job is to clear the first hurdle, not to tell the story of your life",
        "Do not be a nice to have. A role that can be given up is the first one that gets given up",
        "Build relationships with headhunters and agencies, and keep them warm over time rather than only when you need something",
        "Follow the companies that have just raised, the meetups, and the communities where people actually talk",
      ],
      link: { t: "Why you should stop looking for a job", u: "/insights/stop-looking-for-a-job" },
